<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Hilbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David Hilbert was a German mathematician and philosopher of mathematics. Born in or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, he studied and taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a> and in 1895, through the intervention of Felix Klein, became Professor of Mathematics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a>, which became the leading institution in the mathematical world. He worked on invariant theory, algebraic number theory, the foundations of geometry, integral equations and the foundations of mathematics, and was a co-founder of proof theory and mathematical logic. He defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-cantor">Georg Cantor</a>'s set theory, and in 1900 he presented a collection of problems that set a course for 20th-century research.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-hilbert.jpg" alt="David Hilbert" width="330" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilbert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
